Reverse the list by walking it once and flipping each node's `next` pointer to point at the node before it. You need three pointers to do that without losing the rest of the list.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node *reverse(Node *head) {
    Node *prev = NULL;
    Node *curr = head;

    while (curr != NULL) {
        Node *next = curr->next;  // save the rest of the list
        curr->next = prev;        // flip the pointer
        prev = curr;              // advance prev
        curr = next;              // advance curr
    }
    return prev;                  // prev is the new head
}
```

**How it works**

For the list `1 -> 2 -> 3`:

| Step | prev | curr | Result so far |
|------|------|------|---------------|
| start | NULL | 1 | |
| 1 | 1 | 2 | `1 -> NULL` |
| 2 | 2 | 3 | `2 -> 1 -> NULL` |
| 3 | 3 | NULL | `3 -> 2 -> 1 -> NULL` |

When `curr` reaches `NULL`, `prev` points at the old last node, which is now the head.

**Usage**

```c
head = reverse(head);   // reassign, because the head changes
```

This runs in O(n) time and O(1) extra space. It reverses the list in place and allocates nothing.

**Recursive version**

```c
Node *reverse_rec(Node *head) {
    if (head == NULL || head->next == NULL)
        return head;

    Node *new_head = reverse_rec(head->next);
    head->next->next = head;   // make the next node point back at us
    head->next = NULL;
    return new_head;
}
```

This is also O(n) time, but it uses O(n) stack space. A very long list can overflow the stack, so I'd use the iterative version in practice.

**Pitfall:** the function returns the new head, so you have to assign it back. If you ignore the return value, your original `head` pointer will point at the last node of the reversed list and the list will look like it has one element.

If you'd rather not reassign, take a `Node **head` and update `*head` inside the function.

